  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/921338bafb4c56c560502d2ec526703031b0261a5f9c71fcdd0d44058920787e.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?",
  "worked_solution": "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/f5b06b3481472b5be7f4d71da1df3192962c383059a1e7fb36f98426058738ae.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift $y=u(-t)$ vertically upwards by $2$ units, giving $y=u(-t)+2.$\n\n- There is no horizontal shift or vertical scaling.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=u(-t)+2.$",
